r federal  delegation  and all the researchers and scientists  but most importantly those individuals  that volunteered to be in the trial for  the vaccine  it really is a life changer and i see  hope on the horizon and  things moving very quickly for our  community so with that i'm open any  questions you may have  um  so we have an entire process here at umc  it's very thorough well thought out  and follows all the cdc and health  district guidelines so  in fact i will uh let me  get here in my pocket here this is  actually the first vial  that was actually uh given today this is  empty  it's already been utilized but in this  vial we're able to get  five doses of the uh vaccine out to our  employees so it's 1.5 milliliters  so we break that down in 0.3 micrograms  and we're able to dispense this very  quickly  within about 15 minutes to five  employees so  again this is the first vial that's been  administered in the state of nevada  so just to break it down each file  correct so we will uh we'll break down  those vials and those doses  they come in 975  vials in a frozen block so we we do is  systematically break that block up  we dethaw them um takes about 30 minutes  but if we don't dethaun they can stay in  the freezer for up to six months but we  anticipate  uh thawing all the vaccines that we have  here and rolling that out in the next  seven  to eight days  yeah we have about 4 000 employees here  at umc  at umc alone but we also vaccin  vaccinate our  partners at unlv medicine on our  community doctor so  you can add another about 800 on that  number so we'll do close to 5000  over the next seven to eight days again  it's uh  front line workers initially those that  are actually treating patients  actively treating patients we have about  129 patients  in umc currently that were treating  actively for covid  and so a lot of that staff was here  early this morning  and then 21 days later they'll get the  second vaccination the booster  uh and then we're tracking that very  closely so  but again very thorough process that we  have inside  uh we we go through a lot of screening  questions making sure  people are immune compromised or  blood thinners or pregnant or  breastfeeding those type of things so  and then we'll actually administer the  vaccine we have them wait for 15 minutes  to make sure that there's no side  effects or reactions  we have not had any uh yet today i'm  very encouraged and the staff are back  at the bedside so it's going very  smoothly today and  i just want to say thank you to the umc  team to making it so smooth and  um really effortless they are doing a  great job and lots of excitement and  like i said  lots of tears this morning because there  is the solution is here  so we have once we thought we have five  days five days to  to administer the vaccine so time is is  critical and of the essence so we're  moving very quickly and uh  trying not to push up against that five  days but they're thawing uh defaulting  fast as we can and work the demand is  there so  uh we have no problems to meet in that  five day time frame and is it mandatory  for your staff  or is it or is it voluntary it is  voluntary highly encouraged um  we have a lot of interest so  but we're tracking every employee that's  had the vaccine initial and then the  second booster  and following them to make sure that  they come back and make sure that  they're fully  um protected and uh but again  not mandatory um not mandatory in the  state or the country yet but  we're probably gonna see greater than 90  acceptance rate  mary  so no so what we have mary today is uh  we got a call very early on  we're really expecting to roll this out  wednesday  uh but we got a call from the health  district and said  it's here uh i said we're ready and  let's get it rolling so  this all happened within a matter of  hours  it was transported from the health  district right around 11  10 today 11 10 a.m we started  vaccinating our first employer around 11  45  as you can see that that dethawing  process and then administering to our  first  frontline workers so so not anything  related to walgreens or cvs  this is solely healthcare workers at  this point tier one  are they um able to get the vaccine  while they're  in the hospital and if they are when  would they be able to get it back  yeah we're looking at all those patients  that may be compromised  vulnerable within that tier one as you  know nursing home patients  are the most vulnerable so we'll be  working very closely with the nursing  home  community and the health district to be  able to roll that out to make sure that  we're protecting the healthcare workers  in nursing homes  but also in patients in the nursing  homes and we'll also be looking at our  patients that are in umc as well  that'll we need to get through the  healthcare workers first obviously we're  in protective equipment  doing all the therapeutics and  treatments so we're doing really well at  the hospital and  again just very grateful for the time  the state gave us to be able to prepare  for this historic day uh it's here and  uh we're looking good we're very excited  so uh so yeah umc we have to keep this  in deep storage  so negative 71 73 degrees  we're able to receive a lot of the  vaccine so it'll be coming directly  to us after this first initial wave from  the health district  and uh again they'll be coming daily so  i don't anticipate uh  any run on sup the vaccine or being  short at all it's  it's moving very quickly and again very  appreciative to all those that  have been able to get it here in our  community have there been any lessons  in the near future in the process you  know i i like to say we've learned a ton  and  we're fixing a lot of things in the  hospital in the vaccine process but  you know we've been preparing for weeks  we have a great team here at umc  and uh great partnership with the health  district so  uh you know as we speak they're they're  shipping more to us so we're uh  um it's in the it's in the process but  you know we're working out some  some efficiency trying to get quicker  and faster on it but uh  everybody was excited and again a very  pleasant surprise this morning to be  able to start rolling out  the vaccination to our employees before  before noon  they were the first people in nevada  yes yep the first vaccine was given here  to a umc nurse that's uh treated many  many covet patients  in our icu uh it was a great story many  tears in our eyes and uh  and she went back right back to work so  uh it's not over  we still have a long road and uh just  you know really uh  stress to our community please continue  to do the things that  we've been asking you to do there's  light at the end of the tunnel  very appreciated that the governor kept  us in a pause and that we didn't roll  back any more restrictions  he was here this morning saw the process  uh was very pleased and um  but uh yeah that's uh that the first  first uh  person in nevada was vaccinated and was  an employee uh umc a nurse in the icu  did the governor also receive a vaccine  no no he is uh  he is waiting just like i am and we're  going to let everybody  get going first we are we will be the  last and uh he was just a very  appreciative the hard work of the  employees here at umc  uh as you know he's this has been his  hospital for many many years  he knows us well and just wanted to come  out and thank the staff and those that  were given the vaccine in the health  district  you know i think we're gonna it's gonna  take a little while to see the effects  uh you know  i think the key is protecting our front  line healthcare workers  so they have a little bit more peace of  mind that the the vaccine  is is uh taking effect and and we're  gonna be tracking that very closely with  side effects and  how they're doing the hospital is about  88 full  um about 32 percent of our patients are  coveted  uh but umc we do a lot of testing 